From c96d19c520cd770d76dda528d45e36ef4f77c77a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NanoLLM <nanollm@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda: kernels and graphs for Gemma 4 E4B decoding, with a
 switch for each change

The CUDA decode of Gemma 4 E4B (Q4_0 weights, 42 layers, ~1100 kernels a token) spends 75 % of its time in the Q4_0
mat-vec, which reads the weights at 78-95 % of the card's bandwidth, and 25 % in ~700 small kernels of 1-5 us each. These
changes take the kernel time of a token on an RTX 3070 Ti Laptop (cc 8.6) from 9.62 to 8.45 ms (-12 %) and of a draft's
verification from 9.93 to 9.07 ms (-9 %), with the same tokens as plain decoding (below). Every change has a switch,
GGML_CUDA_NANO_<NAME> in the environment or ggml_backend_cuda_nano_set from the host program (nano.cuh), so the build with
and without it is measured in one DLL; the backend's features list NANOLLM (the number of this patch) and the switches.

- GRAPH_SHAPES: the CUDA graph is cached under the first node of the ggml graph, which every batch size of one model
  shares, so a decode that alternates between batch sizes ran eagerly and captured again after every second call. The key
  has the shape (node count, rows of the first and last node) too: one graph per batch size, at most 32 per context.
  MTP with a minimum draft probability on the RTX: 130 -> 147 tok/s.
- MMVQ_NWARPS, MMVQ_NWARPS_N: the block of the Q4_0 mat-vec as warps*10 + rows, per number of columns. One warp per row
  for the wide K = 2560 matrices (the feed-forward's gate and up, the per-layer projection) takes the fused gate/up of one
  token from 84 to 71 us (29 MB: 348 -> 413 GB/s) and the gate or up of four tokens from 41 to 38 us. The number of warps is
  the same for every column count (the order of a row's partial sums depends on it, and a token verified in a batch must get
  the bits it gets alone); rows per block are free. Only cc 8.6 is measured, other cards keep upstream's block.
- vecdotq.cuh: the Q4_0 dot rounds its products and difference one by one. Which product of "a*b - c*d" a compiler fuses
  depends on how it schedules the kernel around it, and the fused gate/up of one token and the two mat-vecs of a draft's
  verification came out with other low bits once the blocks differed; the model's int8 activations make a rounding visible.
- NORM_LEAN: rms_norm * weight (+ residual) (* the layer's output scale) of a row of up to 4096 columns in one block of one
  float4 per thread, and the same for the channels of a 3-D input (the per-layer norms), so that a row is normalized by the
  same kernel for one token and for four. -0.3 ms a token.
- Q8_CACHE, NORM_Q8: the q8_1 of an activation is kept for the rest of the graph evaluation under its tensor (the context's
  own 8 MiB, which never moves: a CUDA graph keeps the addresses it captured), so q, k and v quantize their input once,
  and the input norms write it themselves (rms_norm_mul_lean_q8_1_f32: amax and the quants as quantize_q8_1 computes them).
  132 of 302 quantize launches a token.
- GELU_MUL: gelu(x) * y in one launch, Gemma 4's per-layer embedding gate.
- FUSE_BATCH (off): the gate/up GEGLU fusion for 2-4 tokens; the fused kernel takes 156 us (89 with a one-warp block) against
  87 for the two mat-vecs and the GEGLU launch it replaces.
- FA_VEC (off): the vector flash attention kernel for one token on Ampere, as upstream picks it from Ada; no gain on cc 8.6.

tests: the Q4_0 matrices of Gemma 4 E4B in test-backend-ops (mat-vec 1-4 and 8 columns, the 2560-wide norms with and
without the output scale); test-nano-batch-invariance, which evaluates them with 1-4 columns and compares the first column
bit for bit, also for the gate and up with the GEGLU.
