+// NanoLLM (GGML_CUDA_NANO_MMVQ_NWARPS, _N): the shape of the block that runs the K loop of a Q4_0 mat-vec, as warps*10 +
+// rows per block; 0 is upstream's (4 warps; 1 row for one token, 2 for 2-4). A value >= 0 forces it for the matrices of one
+// token (NWARPS) or of 2-4 (NWARPS_N), -1 (the default) is the table below.
+//
+// What the shape changes in the result is the order in which a row's partial sums are added: the lanes of one warp, and
+// then the warps of the block. So a matrix must have the same number of warps whatever the number of columns, or a token
+// verified in a batch of four gets other rounding than the same token decoded alone, and the model's int8 activations make
+// a rounding visible (a draft's verification would no longer reproduce plain decoding: native_invariance_test.go). The
+// number of rows per block does not touch the order and is free.
+//
+// The table is one rule for every column count: the wide matrices of Gemma 4 E4B with K = 2560 (the feed-forward gate and
+// up, 10240 rows, which one token reads in the fused kernel and a draft in two; the per-layer projection, 10752) run
+// one warp per row (bench/results/.../cuda-kernels-2026-10-21, nsys of llama-bench on an RTX 3070 Ti Laptop, cc 8.6): the
+// fused gate/up of one token takes 71 us instead of 84 (29 MB: 413 GB/s instead of 348), the gate or up of four tokens 38
+// instead of 41. The matrices with one weight and a longer or shorter K are at the card's ceiling already or read too
+// little to matter, and keep upstream's block. Other compute capabilities are unmeasured and keep upstream's choice.
+static int mmvq_nano_shape(int cc, int ncols_dst, int blocks_per_row_x, int nrows_x) {
+    const int v = ggml_cuda_nano_get(ncols_dst == 1 ? GGML_CUDA_NANO_MMVQ_NWARPS : GGML_CUDA_NANO_MMVQ_NWARPS_N);
